  "winner_texts": {
    "1": "The dilemma is solved!\n\n**Recommendation:** Ignore the call.\n\nHere's why:\n\n1. **Respect for others**: In a cinema, you are sharing the space with dozens or even hundreds of\nother viewers who have come to enjoy the movie. Answering your phone will disrupt their experience\nand create a disturbance.\n2. **Prioritizing the event**: You've chosen to watch this movie, and it's likely an important\nouting for you. Ignoring the call shows that you're committed to enjoying the film without\ndistractions.\n3. **Missed moments are recoverable**: While answering your phone might lead to missing a crucial\npart of the movie, you can always rewind or replay the scene when the movie ends. Alternatively, if\nit's an ongoing conversation, you can catch up with the caller later.\n4. **No emergency is life-threatening**: Phone calls in public spaces often stem from non-essential\nissues, such as solicitation, spam, or a wrong number.\n\nIn contrast, answering your phone in a cinema may lead to:\n\n* A lengthy and distracting conversation\n* Other viewers' frustration and potential complaint to the theater management\n* Your own distraction, potentially impacting your overall movie experience\n\nTherefore, I strongly recommend ignoring your ringing phone and letting others enjoy the film\nwithout disturbance. If it's an emergency or truly important call, it'll likely be a repeat caller;\nif not, you can always check your voicemails or messages later.",
    "12": "The eternal dilemma!\n\nMy recommendation is to silence your phone and ignore the call. Here's why:\n\n1. **Respect for others**: You're in a cinema with many other people who have paid to watch the\nmovie, just like you. Answering the call would cause a distraction, potentially interrupting their\nviewing experience and disturbing those around you.\n2. **The impact of missing content is limited**: While it's frustrating to miss a crucial part of\nthe movie, the consequences are relatively minor compared to disrupting others or answering an\nunknown call that might not be urgent.\n3. **Silencing your phone is easier than answering**: It only takes a second to silence your phone\nand put it on \"do not disturb\" mode or send the call to voicemail. This way, you won't miss any\nimportant calls when you're out of the cinema either.\n4. **Important communication will wait**: If someone is calling you urgently, they'll likely leave a\nmessage or try to contact you again after the movie. In this case, answering the call would have\nbeen a nice-to-know situation rather than a must-answer emergency.\n\nIn contrast, missing an important call might lead to inconvenience or temporary discomfort, but it\nwon't cause significant harm. Disrupting others in a public setting can be more damaging and create\nunnecessary tension.\n\nSo, my advice is to silence your phone and let the movie (and those around you) continue\nuninterrupted!"
